Identifying gems is not of earth-shattering importance.

I don't waste any time or scrolls identifying gems.  It's usually
enough to just know whether they are glass or not.  They're worth
the same in the end either way.  As long as you can determine
which are not fakes, you are set.

Hint: Fake gems are all made of glass, and are therefore fragile.
There are glass fakes of each color, but once you've found those,
you don't have to worry. (i.e. once you've found the red glass,
all other red gems are real)

When I start collecting large numbers of gems, I start to see which
are fragile, and discard those.  Naming a whole class (like naming
potions and scrolls) will alert you whenever you see glass of that
color again.  Once all colors are accounted for, I am assured that
every gem I have is valuable.

Of the top of my head, I only can think of two places where
actually IDing a gem is useful:
   When you want to sell it to a shopkeeper for a quick buck.
      If I want his money enough to work for it, there are several
      other things I can sell, especially when I have a pet to steal
      them back for me.  And then there are the pets which really
      make it easy to rob the shopkeepers.
   When you want to toss it to a unicorn for luck.
      I usually settle for other ways of increasing Luck.
      Even so, tossing unidentified gems isn't that much slower than
      tossing identified gems.  It just takes a few more to get to
      Maximum Luck.  And again, a zealous pet can always recover them
      for you.

The only time I ever identify gems is when I list the * as the last
thing on the 'what do you wish to identify' chain.  That way a blessed
scroll might get to them after it ID's stuff I'm really interested in.

